{
 "translation": "Bishan Singh kept muttering, \"O, the many 'gudgud' of the thoughtless 'moong dal' of Pakistan and Hindustan, of the accursed face!\" The preparations for the exchange were complete. The lists of lunatics coming from here to there and there to here had arrived, and the day of the exchange had also been fixed. It was bitterly cold. When the trucks full of Hindu-Sikh lunatics left the Lahore asylum with police escort teams, the concerned officers were also with them. At the Wagah border, superintendents from both sides met each other, and after the initial formalities were completed, the exchange began, which continued all night. Taking the lunatics out of the trucks and handing them over to other officers was a difficult task. Many would not even get out of the trucks. Those who agreed to get out became difficult to manage because they would run off in all directions. Those who were naked, if they were dressed, would tear off the clothes and separate them from their bodies. Someone was swearing, someone was singing. They were fighting amongst themselves, crying, wailing. You couldn't hear anything. The noise of the mad women was separate, and the cold was so biting that teeth were chattering. The majority of the lunatics were not in favor of this transfer because they couldn't understand why they were being uprooted from their place and thrown somewhere else. The few who could think and understand were shouting slogans of 'Pakistan Zindabad' and 'Pakistan Murdabad'. Two or three times, a riot was narrowly avoided because some Muslims and Sikhs got enraged hearing these slogans. When Bishan Singh's turn came and the concerned officer across Wagah started registering his name, he asked, \"Where is Toba Tek Singh? In Pakistan or Hindustan?\" The officer laughed, \"In Pakistan.\" Hearing this, Bishan Singh jumped aside and ran back to his remaining companions. Pakistani soldiers caught him and started taking him to the other side, but he refused to go.",
